('delete /var/run/docker.pid: process with PID 25 is still running')\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\nWhen a Docker-isolated task is resumed in place from its committed container (kill / OOM recovery), the nested Docker daemon cannot start:\\n\\n```\\nfailed to start daemon … delete /var/run/docker.pid: process with PID 25 is still running\\n```\\n\\nObserved on 2026-10-13 (Hive Mind 2.35.8, start-command 0.37.1, task image `konard/hive-mind-dind:2.35.8` = `ghcr.io/link-foundation/box-dind:2.10.4` base) for `Godmy/stylist-svelte#5`, execution `1a664acf-1a63-4973-a4a7-32fc9088c98e`, container `d5173fa1-9c62-4b9b-979f-5748624f3860`: the first attempt was killed by the cgroup OOM killer (3 GB limit), and the in-place resume started from the committed filesystem, which still contains `/var/run/docker.pid` (and possibly `containerd.pid`) from the dead container. In the new container PID 25 is some other process, so dockerd refuses to start. (That resume then also exited 127 on `/claude`, see #2630 / #2887.)\\n\\n## Expected\\n\\n- Before the resumed container starts dockerd, remove stale pid files (`/var/run/docker.pid`, `/var/run/docker/containerd/containerd.pid`, `/var/run/containerd/containerd.pid`) when the PID does not belong to a running dockerd/containerd. A `RUN rm` in `Dockerfile.dind` is not enough: the file comes from the committed snapshot, so do it in the resume entrypoint/handoff script, or exclude `/var/run` from the commit.\\n- If the box-dind entrypoint is the right place, report it to link-foundation/box as well.\\n- Test: commit a container with a running dind, start a new container from it, dockerd starts.\\n\\n\\n__formal_ai_exit=0\\n\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me open https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/3214 and read what it says.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_25513c609db1da46\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"webfetch\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"format\\\":\\\"text\\\",\\\"url\\\":\\\"https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/issues/3214\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_25513c609db1da46\",\"content\":\"In-place resume from a committed dind container: nested dockerd refuses to start (&#39;delete /var/run/docker.pid: process with PID 25 is still running&#39;) · Issue #3214 · link-assistant/hive-mind · GitHub\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n  \\n  \\n  \\n\\n    \\n  \\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n  \\n  \\n\\n  \\n  \\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n\\n  \\n\\n    \\n\\n    \\n\\n      \\n\\n      \\n\\n    \\n    \\n    \\n\\n      \\n  \\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n      \\n\\n\\n\\n        \\n\\n\\n  \\n  \\n  \\n  \\n\\n  \\n\\n      \\n  \\n\\n  \\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n      \\n\\n\\n    \\n  \\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n  \\n  \\n\\n  \\n  \\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n  \\n\\n  \\n    \\n      \\n      \\n\\n    \\n      Skip to content\\n\\n      \\n    \\n      \\n      \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n      \\n\\n          \\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n          \\n\\n                \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n  Navigation MenuSign inAppearance settingsPlatformAI CODE CREATIONGitHub CopilotWrite better code with AIGitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to mergeMCP RegistryIntegrate external toolsDEVELOPER WORKFLOWSActionsAutomate any workflowCodespacesInstant dev environmentsIssuesPlan and track workCode ReviewManage code changesCode QualityEnforce quality at mergeAPPLICATION SECURITYGitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilitiesCode securitySecure your code as you buildSecret protectionStop leaks before they startEXPLOREWhy GitHubDocumentationBlogChangelogMarketplaceView all featuresSolutionsBY COMPANY SIZEEnterprisesSmall and medium teamsStartupsNonprofitsBY USE CASEApp ModernizationDevSecOpsDevOpsCI/CDView all use casesBY INDUSTRYHealthcareFinancial servicesManufacturingGovernmentView all industriesView all solutionsResourcesEXPLORE BY TOPICAISoftware DevelopmentDevOpsSecurityView all topicsEXPLORE BY TYPECustomer storiesEvents &amp; webinarsEbooks &amp; reportsBusiness insightsGitHub SkillsSUPPORT &amp; SERVICESDocumentationCustomer supportCommunity forumTrust centerPartnersView all resourcesOpen SourceCOMMUNITYGitHub SponsorsFund open source developersPROGRAMSSecurity LabMaintainer CommunityGitHub StarsArchive ProgramREPOSITORIESTopicsTrendingCollectionsEnterpriseENTERPRISE SOLUTIONSEnterprise platformAI-powered developer platformAVAILABLE ADD-ONSGitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security featuresCopilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI featuresPremium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 supportPricingSearch/Sign inSign upAppearance settings\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n      \\n  \\n        \\n    \\n\\n        You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session.\\n        You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session.\\n        You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session.\\n\\n      \\n    \\n\\nDismiss alert\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n    \\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n    \\n\\n  \\n    \\n      \\n    \\n\\n    \\n    \\n      \\n      {{ message }}\\n\\n    \\n  \\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n        \\n    \\n      \\n      \\n    \\n\\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n  link-assistanthive-mindRepository navigationCodeIssues139 (139)Pull requests122 (122)ActionsProjectsSecurity and qualityInsightsMore items\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n      \\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n  In-place resume from a committed dind container: nested dockerd refuses to start ('delete /var/run/docker.pid: process with PID 25 is still running') #3214New issueCopy linkNew issueCopy linkOpen#3218OpenIn-place resume from a committed dind container: nested dockerd refuses to start ('delete /var/run/docker.pid: process with PID 25 is still running')#3214#3218Copy linkDescriptionkonardopened on Oct 14, 2026Issue body actionsProblem\\nWhen a Docker-isolated task is resumed in place from its committed container (kill / OOM recovery), the nested Docker daemon cannot start:\\nfailed to start daemon … delete /var/run/docker.pid: process with PID 25 is still running\\n\\nObserved on 2026-10-13 (Hive Mind 2.35.8, start-command 0.37.1, task image konard/hive-mind-dind:2.35.8 = ghcr.io/link-foundation/box-dind:2.10.4 base) for Godmy/stylist-svelte#5, execution 1a664acf-1a63-4973-a4a7-32fc9088c98e, container d5173fa1-9c62-4b9b-979f-5748624f3860: the first attempt was killed by the cgroup OOM killer (3 GB limit), and the in-place resume started from the committed filesystem, which still contains /var/run/docker.pid (and possibly containerd.pid) from the dead container. In the new container PID 25 is some other process, so dockerd refuses to start. (That resume then also exited 127 on /claude, see #2630 / #2887.)\\nExpected\\n\\nBefore the resumed container starts dockerd, remove stale pid files (/var/run/docker.pid, /var/run/docker/containerd/containerd.pid, /var/run/containerd/containerd.pid) when the PID does not belong to a running dockerd/containerd. A RUN rm in Dockerfile.dind is not enough: the file comes from the committed snapshot, so do it in the resume entrypoint/handoff script, or exclude /var/run from the commit.\\nIf the box-dind entrypoint is the right place, report it to link-foundation/box as well.\\nTest: commit a container with a running dind, start a new container from it, dockerd starts.\\nReactions are currently unavailableActivitykonardadded a parent issue on Oct 14, 2026Formal AI Hello World via /claude, /codex, /agent (2026-10-13, 2.35.8): every run failed or falsely succeeded — umbrella (sub-issues) #3215Sign up for free to join this conversation on GitHub. Already have an account? Sign in to commentMetadataMetadataAssigneesNo one assignedLabelsNo labelsNo labelsTypeNo typeProjectsNo projectsMilestoneNo milestoneRelationshipsNone yetDevelopmentNo branches or pull requestsIssue actionsOpen in GitHub Copilot app\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n  \\n\\n  \\n\\n          \\n  Footer\\n\\n  \\n\\n\\n  \\n    \\n      \\n        \\n    \\n\\n\\n      \\n        &copy; 2026 GitHub,&nbsp;Inc.\\n      \\n    \\n\\n    \\n      Footer navigation\\n\\n      \\n\\n\\n          \\n            Terms\\n          \\n\\n          \\n            Privacy\\n          \\n\\n\\n            \\n              Security\\n            \\n\\n            \\n              Status\\n            \\n\\n          \\n            Community\\n          \\n\\n          \\n            Docs\\n          \\n\\n          \\n            Contact\\n          \\n\\n          \\n\\n  \\n    \\n      Manage cookies\\n    \\n  \\n\\n\\n  \\n    \\n      \\n        Do not share my personal information\\n      \\n    \\n  \\n\\n      \\n    \\n  \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n    \\n\\n\\n\\n\\n  \\n    \\n    \\n\\n    \\n      \\n    \\n\\n    \\n    You can’t perform that action at this time.\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me open Dockerfile.dind and read what it says.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_3619f6cf7e1d2f0a\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791955456549/Dockerfile.dind\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_3619f6cf7e1d2f0a\",\"content\":\"<file>\\n00001| # Hive Mind Docker-in-Docker image\\n00002| # Inherits from ghcr.io/link-foundation/box-dind so agents can run Docker-based\\n00003| # tests inside the Hive Mind container while the existing konard/hive-mind image\\n00004| # remains unchanged.\\n00005| #\\n00006| # Runtime:\\n00007| #   docker run --rm --privileged konard/hive-mind-dind:latest docker ps\\n00008| #   docker run --rm --runtime=sysbox-runc konard/hive-mind-dind:latest docker ps\\n00009| #\\n00010| # Box image version: pinned to a specific release for stable, reproducible builds.\\n00011| # To upgrade: update the dind version tag below.\\n00012| # Box 2.3.2 introduced the host-image passthrough allowlist used by the issue #1879\\n00013| # runbook, plus the box#102 fix: the dind entrypoint now WARNS when passthrough is\\n00014| # opted into (DIND_HOST_PASSTHROUGH_IMAGES set) but no host docker socket is mounted,\\n00015| # instead of silently leaving the nested daemon empty (issue #1914).\\n00016| # Box 2.3.4 (box#106 / box PR #107) goes further for issue #1946: the dind\\n00017| # entrypoint now VERIFIES (via `docker image inspect` against the nested daemon)\\n00018| # that each concrete passthrough allowlist entry was actually seeded, and reports\\n00019| # `image preload/passthrough finished WITH WARNINGS` instead of a misleading\\n00020| # `complete` when it was not — so a misconfigured deploy can no longer silently\\n00021| # trigger a ~30 GB re-download of the host image on the first nested `docker run`.\\n00022| # Box 2.4.0 added first-class Docker-outside-of-Docker support (box#110 / box\\n00023| # PR #111): the entrypoint prints the exact `--group-add <gid>` when the box\\n00024| # user cannot reach a mounted host socket, never `chgrp`s that shared socket,\\n00025| # and writes `DIND_READY_FILE` with `complete`/`warnings` so a host can block on\\n00026| # real passthrough readiness instead of racing `docker info`.\\n00027| # The current Box release carries the box#112 fix: one Node.js, one Bun and one\\n00028| # Rust toolchain per image instead of a superseded runtime beside a current one\\n00029| # (issue #2187, item A). See the FROM line below for the exact release.\\n00030| # Latest Box releases: https://github.com/link-foundation/box/releases\\n00031| \\n00032| ARG FORMAL_AI_VERSION=0.353.1\\n00033| # Bookworm's glibc 2.36 remains compatible with the Ubuntu 24.04 Box runtime.\\n00034| FROM rust:1.99-slim-bookworm AS formal-ai-builder\\n00035| ARG FORMAL_AI_VERSION\\n00036| # Formal AI 0.333.0-0.338.0 reached OpenSSL (formal-ai -> web-search ->\\n00037| # web-capture -> reqwest with default features -> native-tls -> openssl-sys), so\\n00038| # the builder needs pkg-config and the OpenSSL headers; rust:slim ships neither\\n00039| # and the openssl-sys build script aborts the install. Reported upstream as\\n00040| # link-assistant/formal-ai#988 and fixed in 0.339.0 (its Cargo.lock no longer\\n00041| # contains openssl-sys), but the root causes are still open upstream\\n00042| # (link-assistant/web-capture#151, link-foundation/browser-commander#77), so the\\n00043| # packages stay as defense in depth: if a future release drags openssl-sys back\\n00044| # in, OPENSSL_STATIC links libssl into the binary and the copy into the Ubuntu\\n00045| # 24.04 runtime below stays independent of the runtime's OpenSSL soname; when\\n00046| # openssl-sys is absent both are inert (see docs/case-studies/issue-2146).\\n00047| RUN apt-get update && \\\\\\n00048|     apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends pkg-config libssl-dev && \\\\\\n00049|     rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\\n00050| ENV OPENSSL_STATIC=1\\n00051| RUN cargo install formal-ai --version \\\"${FORMAL_AI_VERSION}\\\" --locked\\n00052| \\n00053| # Box 2.10.4 retains the box#112 one-toolchain invariant and the base\\n00054| # runtimes: Node.js v24.21.0, Bun 1.4.2 and stable Rust 1.98.1. Box PR #120\\n00055| # repaired the Docker Hub multi-arch publishing failure reported in box#119;\\n00056| # both registries now carry amd64 and arm64 manifests. Keep GHCR, Box's registry\\n00057| # of record, as the reproducible source for this pin. Box #129 also adds file\\n00058| # for validating downloaded attachments. Box 2.10.4 enables Docker live\\n00059| # restore in the nested DinD daemon (box#131).\\n00060| FROM ghcr.io/link-foundation/box-dind:2.10.4\\n00061| ARG HIVE_MIND_VERSION=latest\\n00062| # Task-container prerequisite installs stay within the workspace (formal-ai#1159).\\n00063| ENV FORMAL_AI_INSTALL_GRANT=workspace\\n00064| \\n00065| # --- Environment variables ---\\n00066| ENV HOME=/home/box\\n00067| ENV HIVE_MIND_IMAGE_VARIANT=dind\\n00068| # Release builds pass the exact published package version here. Bake it as the\\n00069| # default child isolation image tag so a parent started via :latest still runs\\n00070| # Docker-isolated tasks on the same immutable release image.\\n00071| ENV HIVE_MIND_DOCKER_ISOLATION_IMAGE_TAG=\\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\"\\n00072| # Nested-Docker storage driver. MUST be a copy-on-write driver for these images.\\n00073| #\\n00074| # The Hive Mind images are multiple gigabytes with many layers. `vfs` performs\\n00075| # NO copy-on-write: it materializes a full, independent copy of the entire\\n00076| # filesystem for every layer, so the on-disk footprint is the *sum* of all\\n00077| # cumulative layer sizes — many times the image size. On a >30 GB image that\\n00078| # overflows the disk and the first isolated `--isolation docker` task dies with\\n00079| # `failed to register layer: no space left on device` (issue #1914 reopen).\\n00080| #\\n00081| # `fuse-overlayfs` is the right default: it is copy-on-write (so the image\\n00082| # costs roughly its real size once, not a per-layer multiple) AND it works\\n00083| # overlay-on-overlay, which is the compatibility `vfs` was originally chosen\\n00084| # for — `overlay2` can fail on the overlay-backed hosts our deploys run on.\\n00085| # box-dind ships the `fuse-overlayfs` binary and Hive Mind launches the dind\\n00086| # container with `--privileged`, so /dev/fuse is available at runtime.\\n00087| #\\n00088| # NOTE: box-dind already auto-detects a driver (overlay2 -> fuse-overlayfs ->\\n00089| # vfs, with graceful fallback). Setting this env var OVERRIDES that detection\\n00090| # and pins one driver. The previous `vfs` value here defeated the auto-detect\\n00091| # and forced the no-copy-on-write driver — the exact cause of the #1914 reopen.\\n00092| # We pin `fuse-overlayfs` (not blank/auto) for determinism: it skips the\\n00093| # overlay2-on-overlay attempt that tends to fail in our nested deploys and\\n00094| # guarantees a copy-on-write daemon.\\n00095| #\\n00096| # Override only if you know your host: `-e DIND_STORAGE_DRIVER=overlay2` is\\n00097| # faster where nested overlay mounts are supported; `-e DIND_STORAGE_DRIVER=vfs`\\n00098| # is a last-resort compatibility fallback but uses many times the disk and is\\n00099| # the configuration that caused issue #1914.\\n00100| ENV DIND_STORAGE_DRIVER=\\\"fuse-overlayfs\\\"\\n00101| ENV NVM_DIR=\\\"/home/box/.nvm\\\"\\n00102| ENV PYENV_ROOT=\\\"/home/box/.pyenv\\\"\\n00103| ENV BUN_INSTALL=\\\"/home/box/.bun\\\"\\n00104| ENV DENO_INSTALL=\\\"/home/box/.deno\\\"\\n00105| ENV CARGO_HOME=\\\"/home/box/.cargo\\\"\\n00106| ENV GOROOT=\\\"/home/box/.go\\\"\\n00107| ENV GOPATH=\\\"/home/box/.go/path\\\"\\n00108| ENV SDKMAN_DIR=\\\"/home/box/.sdkman\\\"\\n00109| ENV PERLBREW_ROOT=\\\"/home/box/.perl5\\\"\\n00110| ENV RBENV_ROOT=\\\"/home/box/.rbenv\\\"\\n00111| \\n00112| # Quiet, deterministic Claude Code defaults for autonomous solve runs (issue #1642)\\n00113| # The two memory switches are policy, not cosmetics: a hive-mind task keeps no\\n00114| # memory a reviewer cannot see, so the repository stays the only memory (issue #2178)\\n00115| # The marketplace switch keeps the global config minimal: the official plugin\\n00116| # marketplace must never be auto-installed into a task (issue #2190)\\n00117| # The five CLASSIFIER/RECAP/NARRATION/SUGGESTION/TOOL_USE_SUMMARIES switches are\\n00118| # pinned to 0 rather than left unset because Claude Code falls through to a remote\\n00119| # rollout flag when they are undefined: they are auxiliary model calls nobody in an\\n00120| # autonomous run reads, and they bill against the same limit as the task (issue #2236)\\n00121| # Compaction is deliberately NOT disabled here — DISABLE_AUTO_COMPACT and\\n00122| # DISABLE_COMPACT must never appear in this image (issue #2236)\\n00123| ENV CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1 \\\\\\n00124|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_ORG_MEMORY=1 \\\\\\n00125|     CLAUDE_CODE_CLASSIFIER_SUMMARY=0 \\\\\\n00126|     CLAUDE_CODE_EMIT_TOOL_USE_SUMMARIES=0 \\\\\\n00127|     CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_NARRATION=0 \\\\\\n00128|     CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_PROMPT_SUGGESTION=0 \\\\\\n00129|     CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_REMOTE_RECAP=0 \\\\\\n00130|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_OFFICIAL_MARKETPLACE_AUTOINSTALL=1 \\\\\\n00131|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_CRON=1 \\\\\\n00132|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE=1 \\\\\\n00133|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_CLAUDE_MDS=1 \\\\\\n00134|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_FAST_MODE=1 \\\\\\n00135|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_FEEDBACK_SURVEY=1 \\\\\\n00136|     CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_MOUSE=1 \\\\\\n00137|     CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_AWAY_SUMMARY=0 \\\\\\n00138|     CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TASKS=1 \\\\\\n00139|     CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_TOOL_USE_CONCURRENCY=4 \\\\\\n00140|     CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN=1 \\\\\\n00141|     DISABLE_FEEDBACK_COMMAND=1\\n00142| \\n00143| ENV OPAM_SWITCH_PREFIX=\\\"/home/box/.opam/default\\\"\\n00144| ENV CAML_LD_LIBRARY_PATH=\\\"/home/box/.opam/default/lib/stublibs:/home/box/.opam/default/lib/ocaml/stublibs:/home/box/.opam/default/lib/ocaml\\\"\\n00145| ENV OCAML_TOPLEVEL_PATH=\\\"/home/box/.opam/default/lib/toplevel\\\"\\n00146| \\n00147| ENV PATH=\\\"/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/opt/php@8.3/bin:/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/opt/php@8.3/sbin:/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin:/home/box/.pyenv/bin:/home/box/.pyenv/shims:/home/box/.rbenv/bin:/home/box/.rbenv/shims:/home/box/.swift/usr/bin:/home/box/.elan/bin:/home/box/.opam/default/bin:/home/box/.local/bin:/home/box/.cargo/bin:/home/box/.deno/bin:/home/box/.bun/bin:/home/box/.go/bin:/home/box/.go/path/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin\\\"\\n00148| \\n00149| USER box\\n00150| WORKDIR /home/box\\n00151| \\n00152| # Use bash for every subsequent RUN. nvm is sourced below and the build steps\\n00153| # further down already use bash-only syntax (`&>`), which /bin/sh (dash) parses\\n00154| # as \\\"run in background, then redirect\\\" — silently turning those guards into\\n00155| # no-ops. Declaring the shell here instead of at the end of the file makes the\\n00156| # steps run as they read (issue #2187).\\n00157| SHELL [\\\"/bin/bash\\\", \\\"-c\\\"]\\n00158| \\n00159| # The solver verifies downloaded attachments with file before opening them.\\n00160| # Box's runtime does not guarantee this utility (issue #2687).\\n00161| RUN command -v file >/dev/null || HOMEBREW_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 brew install file\\n00162| \\n00163| # --- Current Node.js and Bun (issue #2187) ---\\n00164| # Box bases up to 2.4.0 installed Node.js 20 (`nvm install 20` in box's\\n00165| # ubuntu/24.04/js/install.sh) plus whatever Bun was current when the base was\\n00166| # built, so every derived image inherited runtimes well behind the workloads.\\n00167| # Hive Mind itself declares `engines.node >= 24`, and tasks on repositories that\\n00168| # need something newer used to download their own node/bun into /tmp on every\\n00169| # run and leave the copy behind — a second accumulation of versions on top of\\n00170| # the image's own. Reported upstream as link-foundation/box#112 and fixed in\\n00171| # box 2.7.0. The current 2.10.4 base keeps one version per language. When a\\n00172| # pin below differs from the base, replace the runtime and repoint the default\\n00173| # before pruning superseded Node.js versions (the prune behavior is covered\\n00174| # by tests/test-issue-2187-runtime-versions.mjs).\\n00175| #\\n00176| # The layer is kept anyway, because it is the floor rather than the source of\\n00177| # the runtime. The base's versions are box's decision and can move under us in\\n00178| # either direction; `engines.node` is ours. Installing here means a base bump\\n00179| # can never silently drop the image below the floor, and the prune loop still\\n00180| # collapses whatever the base ships to a single version. The pin wins over the\\n00181| # base — a base shipping something NEWER would be pruned back to the pin — so\\n00182| # when box moves, bump these ARGs to match instead of deleting the block.\\n00183| #\\n00184| # Pin the runtimes here (bump these ARGs like any other pin):\\n00185| #   - HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION must stay >= the `engines.node` floor in package.json;\\n00186| #     tests/test-issue-2187-runtime-versions.mjs fails the build otherwise.\\n00187| #   - HIVE_MIND_BUN_VERSION is installed OVER the inherited binary, so ~/.bun keeps one\\n00188| #     bun rather than accumulating versions.\\n00189| #\\n00190| # The nvm root is left with exactly ONE node version: the base's global npm\\n00191| # packages are re-installed under the new version AT THEIR EXISTING VERSIONS\\n00192| # (pinning matters — ~/.cache/ms-playwright already holds the browser builds\\n00193| # matching the inherited playwright, so an unpinned upgrade would leave the CLI\\n00194| # pointing at browsers the image does not have), then every other version\\n00195| # directory is removed. `.node-bin`, `nvm use default` and a bare `node` then\\n00196| # all resolve to the same, newest runtime (issue #2187, item A).\\n00197| ARG HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION=26.11.1\\n00198| ARG HIVE_MIND_BUN_VERSION=1.4.3\\n00199| RUN set -e && \\\\\\n00200|     . \\\"$NVM_DIR/nvm.sh\\\" && \\\\\\n00201|     PREVIOUS_GLOBAL_LIB=\\\"$(dirname \\\"$(dirname \\\"$(command -v node)\\\")\\\")/lib/node_modules\\\" && \\\\\\n00202|     GLOBAL_SPECS=\\\"\\\" && \\\\\\n00203|     for package_json in \\\"$PREVIOUS_GLOBAL_LIB\\\"/*/package.json \\\"$PREVIOUS_GLOBAL_LIB\\\"/@*/*/package.json; do \\\\\\n00204|       [ -f \\\"$package_json\\\" ] || continue; \\\\\\n00205|       spec=\\\"$(node -p 'const pkg = require(process.argv[1]); pkg.name + \\\"@\\\" + pkg.version' \\\"$package_json\\\")\\\"; \\\\\\n00206|       case \\\"$spec\\\" in npm@*|corepack@*) continue ;; esac; \\\\\\n00207|       GLOBAL_SPECS=\\\"$GLOBAL_SPECS $spec\\\"; \\\\\\n00208|     done && \\\\\\n00209|     echo \\\"Inherited global npm packages to re-install:${GLOBAL_SPECS:- (none)}\\\" && \\\\\\n00210|     nvm install \\\"${HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION}\\\" && \\\\\\n00211|     nvm alias default \\\"${HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION}\\\" && \\\\\\n00212|     nvm use default && \\\\\\n00213|     if [ -n \\\"$GLOBAL_SPECS\\\" ]; then npm install -g $GLOBAL_SPECS --no-fund --force; fi && \\\\\\n00214|     for version_dir in \\\"$NVM_DIR\\\"/versions/node/*; do \\\\\\n00215|       [ -d \\\"$version_dir\\\" ] || continue; \\\\\\n00216|       if [ \\\"$(basename \\\"$version_dir\\\")\\\" = \\\"v${HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION}\\\" ]; then continue; fi; \\\\\\n00217|       echo \\\"Removing superseded node $(basename \\\"$version_dir\\\")\\\"; \\\\\\n00218|       rm -rf \\\"$version_dir\\\"; \\\\\\n00219|     done && \\\\\\n00220|     curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash -s \\\"bun-v${HIVE_MIND_BUN_VERSION}\\\" && \\\\\\n00221|     node --version && \\\\\\n00222|     npm --version && \\\\\\n00223|     \\\"$BUN_INSTALL/bin/bun\\\" --version\\n00224| \\n00225| # `sort -V | tail -1` (not `ls | head -1`): `ls` sorts lexicographically and\\n00226| # ascending, so once a second Node.js version is installed the plain listing\\n00227| # picks the OLDEST one (and text-sorts \\\"v9\\\" above \\\"v22\\\"). Version-sort and take\\n00228| # the last entry so /home/box/.node-bin always points at the newest node in the\\n00229| # image (issue #2187).\\n00230| RUN NODE_VERSION_DIR=$(ls -d /home/box/.nvm/versions/node/v* 2>/dev/null | sort -V | tail -1) && \\\\\\n00231|     if [ -n \\\"$NODE_VERSION_DIR\\\" ] && [ -d \\\"$NODE_VERSION_DIR/bin\\\" ]; then \\\\\\n00232|       ln -sf \\\"$NODE_VERSION_DIR/bin\\\" /home/box/.node-bin; \\\\\\n00233|     fi\\n00234| \\n00235| ENV PATH=\\\"/home/box/.node-bin:${PATH}\\\"\\n00236| \\n00237| COPY --from=formal-ai-builder /usr/local/cargo/bin/formal-ai /usr/local/bin/formal-ai\\n00238| RUN formal-ai --version\\n00239| \\n00240| # The Box full image includes the Rocq/Coq opam switch data. Keep an explicit\\n00241| # opam binary in ~/.local/bin so verification and interactive use are stable.\\n00242| RUN mkdir -p /home/box/.local/bin && \\\\\\n00243|     ARCH=\\\"$(uname -m)\\\" && \\\\\\n00244|     case \\\"$ARCH\\\" in \\\\\\n00245|       x86_64)  OPAM_ARCH=\\\"x86_64\\\" ;; \\\\\\n00246|       aarch64) OPAM_ARCH=\\\"arm64\\\" ;; \\\\\\n00247|       *)       OPAM_ARCH=\\\"$ARCH\\\" ;; \\\\\\n00248|     esac && \\\\\\n00249|     OPAM_TAG=$(curl -fsSIL -o /dev/null -w '%{url_effective}' https://github.com/ocaml/opam/releases/latest | sed 's|.*/||') && \\\\\\n00250|     curl -fsSL \\\"https://github.com/ocaml/opam/releases/download/${OPAM_TAG}/opam-${OPAM_TAG}-${OPAM_ARCH}-linux\\\" -o /home/box/.local/bin/opam && \\\\\\n00251|     chmod +x /home/box/.local/bin/opam\\n00252| \\n00253| RUN curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh -o /tmp/claude-code-install.sh && \\\\\\n00254|     bash /tmp/claude-code-install.sh && \\\\\\n00255|     rm /tmp/claude-code-install.sh && \\\\\\n00256|     claude --version\\n00257| \\n00258| RUN bun install -g @openai/codex && \\\\\\n00259|     bun install -g @qwen-code/qwen-code && \\\\\\n00260|     bun install -g @google/gemini-cli && \\\\\\n00261|     bun install -g @github/copilot && \\\\\\n00262|     bun install -g opencode-ai\\n00263| \\n00264| # start-command is pinned to 0.37.5: 0.29.1 fixed detached docker\\n00265| # `--status`/`--list` reporting a terminal status (`executed`) with the `-1`\\n00266| # sentinel while the container is still running (link-foundation/start#136,\\n00267| # link-assistant/hive-mind#1939); 0.29.2 (start#138 / start PR #139) records the\\n00268| # docker image-preparation phase (the `docker pull`/dind boot) in the session log,\\n00269| # so `$ --upload-log` no longer returns a near-empty 546-byte log while a multi-GB\\n00270| # image is still pulling. 0.30.1 (start#140 / start PR #141) adds explicit docker\\n00271| # container cleanup policies and removes successful containers by default while\\n00272| # keeping the host-side log, which Hive Mind also enforces at task completion\\n00273| # for defense in depth (issue #1979). 0.30.2 (start#144) surfaces detached docker\\n00274| # `OOMKilled` status and preserves abnormally-terminated container filesystems\\n00275| # under the default cleanup policy — the upstream defense-in-depth half of the\\n00276| # #1990 fix (the primary terminal-completion gate lives in this repo's solve).\\n00277| # 0.30.3 (start#148/#149) reconciles detached Docker `OOMKilled=true` as terminal\\n00278| # in upstream `--status`/`--list`, which directly covers issue #2015.\\n00279| # 0.31.0 (start#154 / start PR #155) adds `--network` / `--network-alias` for the\\n00280| # docker isolation backend. 0.32.0 (start#156 / start PR #157, unblocked for npm\\n00281| # by start#160) makes `--network` repeatable: the first network is passed to\\n00282| # `docker create` and the rest are attached with `docker network connect` before\\n00283| # `docker start`, so a task could join the default bridge AND the `--internal`\\n00284| # Formal AI sidecar network at launch. Hive Mind still performs that same\\n00285| # additive `docker network connect` itself, inside the already-held start gate:\\n00286| # the sequencing is identical, the gate is held anyway for the writable-layer\\n00287| # baseline, and the in-repo attach keeps working (fail-closed) on any\\n00288| # start-command version instead of silently degrading to a single network on\\n00289| # pre-0.32.0 parsers (see docs/case-studies/issue-2146, issue #2146).\\n00290| # 0.33.0 (start#162, #164, #165) closes the three upstream issues this repo filed\\n00291| # from the issue #2189 incident: `--attach` / `--resume <uuid>` / `--resume-all`\\n00292| # re-enter an existing execution — same container, same execution UUID — instead\\n00293| # of forcing a fresh isolated run (start#162); `--status`/`--list` expose\\n00294| # `exitReason` and `memoryExhausted`/`memoryExhaustedReason`, so a V8 heap\\n00295| # self-abort (`FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit`, exit 134/139 with `oomKilled`\\n00296| # false and cgroup `oom_kill` 0) is no longer indistinguishable from a forced\\n00297| # kill (start#164); and the exit markers are detected in a bounded 64 KiB log-tail\\n00298| # window instead of buffering the whole log (start#165). Hive Mind consumes all\\n00299| # three and keeps its own log-marker kill classification and streaming sanitizer\\n00300| # as defense in depth, so behaviour degrades gracefully on an older `$` binary\\n00301| # (see docs/case-studies/issue-2189, issue #2189).\\n00302| # 0.34.0 persists the terminal state and post-mortem of detached Docker\\n00303| # executions, so status queries stop inventing a new finish time on each read.\\n00304| # 0.35.0 (start#176, filed from issue #2408) re-applies the stopped container's\\n00305| # resource limits — including ones set later with `docker update` — when\\n00306| # `--resume <id> -- <cmd>` commits it and starts the `-resume-N` container, so a\\n00307| # resource-limited OOM recovery can stay in the same execution UUID and log\\n00308| # (see src/session-kill-resume.in-place.lib.mjs). Its new `--on-kill-resume`\\n00309| # is not used yet. 0.35.1 fixes start#178: it no longer treats the sticky\\n00310| # `OOMKilled` flag as a kill when the main process exits 0-127 on its own\\n00311| # (see docs/case-studies/issue-2408). 0.35.2 fixes start#180: the sticky\\n00312| # flag no longer determines the exit reason for ordinary exits. It also\\n00313| # samples the task cgroup OOM counters/peak (#182) and supports cancellable\\n00314| # recovery delays (#181). Hive Mind consumes the counters but keeps its own\\n00315| # recovery policy so deliberate solve stops and attempt budgets are honoured.\\n00316| # 0.35.3 stops inferring OOM scope from process/event ratios (#185). 0.35.4 fixes\\n00317| # start#187 (filed from issue #2498): an explicit `--resume` moves the previous\\n00318| # attempt's memory evidence into `attemptHistory` and records the new attempt's\\n00319| # start time, log byte offset and lifecycle boundary. 0.36.0 adds opt-in Docker\\n00320| # resource limits and requires exit-time evidence before an OOM verdict. 0.37.0\\n00321| # adds copy-free Docker command resumes, persistent task labels and faithful\\n00322| # shell argument and exit-code handling. 0.37.1 observes Docker CPU limits\\n00323| # applied after launch and recovers CPU penalty monitors after crashes. 0.37.2\\n00324| # attributes exit 137 to recent Docker OOM events when the last cgroup sample\\n00325| # misses the final kill; 0.37.3-0.37.5 only change the release pipeline.\\n00326| # `@link-assistant/agent` is pinned to current 0.26.13. Version 0.26.1 stopped the\\n00327| # unbounded snapshot leak of issue #2186. Up to 0.26.0 `Snapshot.track()` built a\\n00328| # standalone git object store per project — keyed on the worktree's root commit,\\n00329| # with no `objects/info/alternates` and no garbage collection — so a harness that\\n00330| # runs the agent in throwaway `git init` checkouts left one full ~270 MB copy of\\n00331| # the repository behind per invocation: 115 orphaned stores / 31 GB in a single\\n00332| # task, growing at ~5 GB/h, in `~/.local/share/link-assistant-agent/snapshot/`\\n00333| # where none of Hive Mind's `/tmp`-scoped disk checks could see it.\\n00334| # link-assistant/agent#298 (PR #300) shares the repository's objects and prunes\\n00335| # projects whose recorded worktree is gone. `src/agent.lib.mjs` enforces the same\\n00336| # floor at runtime, so the pin and the guard cannot drift apart.\\n00337| RUN echo \\\"Installing @link-assistant/hive-mind@${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\" && \\\\\\n00338|     bun install -g \\\"@link-assistant/hive-mind@${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\" && \\\\\\n00339|     if [ \\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\" != \\\"latest\\\" ]; then \\\\\\n00340|       test \\\"$(hive --version)\\\" = \\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\"; \\\\\\n00341|     fi && \\\\\\n00342|     bun install -g @link-assistant/claude-profiles && \\\\\\n00343|     bun install -g @link-assistant/agent@0.26.13 && \\\\\\n00344|     bun install -g start-command@0.37.5 && \\\\\\n00345|     bun install -g gh-setup-git-identity && \\\\\\n00346|     bun install -g gh-pull-all && \\\\\\n00347|     bun install -g gh-load-issue && \\\\\\n00348|     bun install -g gh-load-pull-request && \\\\\\n00349|     bun install -g gh-upload-log@latest\\n00350| \\n00351| # Issue #2190: @playwright/cli ships the optional `playwright-cli` Agent Skill\\n00352| # deployed per task by --playwright-skill (default stays Playwright MCP only).\\n00353| RUN npm install -g @playwright/mcp@latest @playwright/cli@latest --no-fund --force\\n00354| \\n00355| # The skill is checked by path: `playwright-cli --help` prints its `Agent skill:`\\n00356| # hint only when CLAUDECODE or COPILOT_CLI is set, so that line is not a\\n00357| # build-time signal (issue #2190).\\n00358| RUN playwright --version && \\\\\\n00359|     npx --no-install @playwright/mcp --help | grep -q -- '--headless' && \\\\\\n00360|     test -f \\\"$(npm root -g)/@playwright/cli/skills/playwright-cli/SKILL.md\\\"\\n00361| \\n00362| RUN if command -v claude &>/dev/null; then \\\\\\n00363|       claude mcp add playwright -s user -- npx -y @playwright/mcp@latest --isolated --headless --no-sandbox --timeout-action=600000 --viewport-size 1920x1080; \\\\\\n00364|     fi\\n00365| \\n00366| RUN if command -v codex &>/dev/null; then \\\\\\n00367|       codex mcp add playwright -- npx -y @playwright/mcp@latest --isolated --headless --no-sandbox --timeout-action=600000 --viewport-size 1920x1080; \\\\\\n00368|     fi\\n00369| \\n00370| # Issue #2190: keep the global Codex configuration minimal. Codex syncs the\\n00371| # \\\"openai-curated-remote\\\" plugin catalog (Superpowers and friends) into\\n00372| # ~/.codex/plugins/cache on every start unless features.remote_plugin is false;\\n00373| # those skills force approval gates and multiply token usage in autonomous runs.\\n00374| RUN if command -v codex &>/dev/null; then \\\\\\n00375|       mkdir -p \\\"$HOME/.codex\\\" && touch \\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\" && \\\\\\n00376|       if grep -q '^\\\\[features\\\\]' \\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\"; then \\\\\\n00377|         sed -i 's/^\\\\[features\\\\]$/[features]\\\\nremote_plugin = false/' \\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\"; \\\\\\n00378|       else \\\\\\n00379|         printf '\\\\n[features]\\\\nremote_plugin = false\\\\n' >> \\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\"; \\\\\\n00380|       fi && \\\\\\n00381|       grep -q '^remote_plugin = false' \\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\" && cat \\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\"; \\\\\\n00382|     fi\\n00383| \\n00384| RUN if command -v claude >/dev/null 2>&1; then \\\\\\n00385|       CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT=\\\"$(claude mcp list 2>&1)\\\" && \\\\\\n00386|       echo \\\"$CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT\\\" && \\\\\\n00387|       echo \\\"$CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(connected|enabled)' && \\\\\\n00388|       ! echo \\\"$CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(pending|disabled|failed|error|disconnected|not[-_[:space:]]+connected|unavailable|timed[-_[:space:]]+out|(^|[^[:alnum:]_-])timeout($|[^[:alnum:]_-]))'; \\\\\\n00389|     fi && \\\\\\n00390|     if command -v codex >/dev/null 2>&1; then \\\\\\n00391|       CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT=\\\"$(codex mcp list 2>&1)\\\" && \\\\\\n00392|       echo \\\"$CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT\\\" && \\\\\\n00393|       echo \\\"$CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(connected|enabled)' && \\\\\\n00394|       ! echo \\\"$CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(pending|disabled|failed|error|disconnected|not[-_[:space:]]+connected|unavailable|timed[-_[:space:]]+out|(^|[^[:alnum:]_-])timeout($|[^[:alnum:]_-]))'; \\\\\\n00395|     fi\\n00396| \\n00397| RUN mkdir -p /home/box/.claude && \\\\\\n00398|     if [ \\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\" != \\\"latest\\\" ]; then \\\\\\n00399|       configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json && \\\\\\n00400|       configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json --verify; \\\\\\n00401|     elif command -v configure-claude >/dev/null 2>&1; then \\\\\\n00402|       configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json && \\\\\\n00403|       configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json --verify; \\\\\\n00404|     else \\\\\\n00405|       echo \\\"configure-claude not present in @link-assistant/hive-mind@latest yet (likely a PR build before the bin is published); skipping baseline - solve re-applies it at runtime\\\"; \\\\\\n00406|     fi\\n00407| \\n\\n(End of file - total 407 lines)\\n</file>\"},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"Let me update e.g for you.\",\"tool_calls\":[{\"id\":\"call_dd231e22a4c1ee5c\",\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"write\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"content\\\":\\\"# Hive Mind Docker-in-Docker image\\\\n# Inherits from ghcr.io/link-foundation/box-dind so agents can run Docker-based\\\\n# tests inside the Hive Mind container while the existing konard/hive-mind image\\\\n# remains unchanged.\\\\n#\\\\n# Runtime:\\\\n#   docker run --rm --privileged konard/hive-mind-dind:latest docker ps\\\\n#   docker run --rm --runtime=sysbox-runc konard/hive-mind-dind:latest docker ps\\\\n#\\\\n# Box image version: pinned to a specific release for stable, reproducible builds.\\\\n# To upgrade: update the dind version tag below.\\\\n# Box 2.3.2 introduced the host-image passthrough allowlist used by the issue #1879\\\\n# runbook, plus the box#102 fix: the dind entrypoint now WARNS when passthrough is\\\\n# opted into (DIND_HOST_PASSTHROUGH_IMAGES set) but no host docker socket is mounted,\\\\n# instead of silently leaving the nested daemon empty (issue #1914).\\\\n# Box 2.3.4 (box#106 / box PR #107) goes further for issue #1946: the dind\\\\n# entrypoint now VERIFIES (via `docker image inspect` against the nested daemon)\\\\n# that each concrete passthrough allowlist entry was actually seeded, and reports\\\\n# `image preload/passthrough finished WITH WARNINGS` instead of a misleading\\\\n# `complete` when it was not — so a misconfigured deploy can no longer silently\\\\n# trigger a ~30 GB re-download of the host image on the first nested `docker run`.\\\\n# Box 2.4.0 added first-class Docker-outside-of-Docker support (box#110 / box\\\\n# PR #111): the entrypoint prints the exact `--group-add <gid>` when the box\\\\n# user cannot reach a mounted host socket, never `chgrp`s that shared socket,\\\\n# and writes `DIND_READY_FILE` with `complete`/`warnings` so a host can block on\\\\n# real passthrough readiness instead of racing `docker info`.\\\\n# The current Box release carries the box#112 fix: one Node.js, one Bun and one\\\\n# Rust toolchain per image instead of a superseded runtime beside a current one\\\\n# (issue #2187, item A). See the FROM line below for the exact release.\\\\n# Latest Box releases: https://github.com/link-foundation/box/releases\\\\n\\\\nARG FORMAL_AI_VERSION=0.353.1\\\\n# Bookworm's glibc 2.36 remains compatible with the Ubuntu 24.04 Box runtime.\\\\nFROM rust:1.99-slim-bookworm AS formal-ai-builder\\\\nARG FORMAL_AI_VERSION\\\\n# Formal AI 0.333.0-0.338.0 reached OpenSSL (formal-ai -> web-search ->\\\\n# web-capture -> reqwest with default features -> native-tls -> openssl-sys), so\\\\n# the builder needs pkg-config and the OpenSSL headers; rust:slim ships neither\\\\n# and the openssl-sys build script aborts the install. Reported upstream as\\\\n# link-assistant/formal-ai#988 and fixed in 0.339.0 (its Cargo.lock no longer\\\\n# contains openssl-sys), but the root causes are still open upstream\\\\n# (link-assistant/web-capture#151, link-foundation/browser-commander#77), so the\\\\n# packages stay as defense in depth: if a future release drags openssl-sys back\\\\n# in, OPENSSL_STATIC links libssl into the binary and the copy into the Ubuntu\\\\n# 24.04 runtime below stays independent of the runtime's OpenSSL soname; when\\\\n# openssl-sys is absent both are inert (see docs/case-studies/issue-2146).\\\\nRUN apt-get update && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends pkg-config libssl-dev && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\\\\nENV OPENSSL_STATIC=1\\\\nRUN cargo install formal-ai --version \\\\\\\"${FORMAL_AI_VERSION}\\\\\\\" --locked\\\\n\\\\n# Box 2.10.4 retains the box#112 one-toolchain invariant and the base\\\\n# runtimes: Node.js v24.21.0, Bun 1.4.2 and stable Rust 1.98.1. Box PR #120\\\\n# repaired the Docker Hub multi-arch publishing failure reported in box#119;\\\\n# both registries now carry amd64 and arm64 manifests. Keep GHCR, Box's registry\\\\n# of record, as the reproducible source for this pin. Box #129 also adds file\\\\n# for validating downloaded attachments. Box 2.10.4 enables Docker live\\\\n# restore in the nested DinD daemon (box#131).\\\\nFROM ghcr.io/link-foundation/box-dind:2.10.4\\\\nARG HIVE_MIND_VERSION=latest\\\\n# Task-container prerequisite installs stay within the workspace (formal-ai#1159).\\\\nENV FORMAL_AI_INSTALL_GRANT=workspace\\\\n\\\\n# --- Environment variables ---\\\\nENV HOME=/home/box\\\\nENV HIVE_MIND_IMAGE_VARIANT=dind\\\\n# Release builds pass the exact published package version here. Bake it as the\\\\n# default child isolation image tag so a parent started via :latest still runs\\\\n# Docker-isolated tasks on the same immutable release image.\\\\nENV HIVE_MIND_DOCKER_ISOLATION_IMAGE_TAG=\\\\\\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\\\\\"\\\\n# Nested-Docker storage driver. MUST be a copy-on-write driver for these images.\\\\n#\\\\n# The Hive Mind images are multiple gigabytes with many layers. `vfs` performs\\\\n# NO copy-on-write: it materializes a full, independent copy of the entire\\\\n# filesystem for every layer, so the on-disk footprint is the *sum* of all\\\\n# cumulative layer sizes — many times the image size. On a >30 GB image that\\\\n# overflows the disk and the first isolated `--isolation docker` task dies with\\\\n# `failed to register layer: no space left on device` (issue #1914 reopen).\\\\n#\\\\n# `fuse-overlayfs` is the right default: it is copy-on-write (so the image\\\\n# costs roughly its real size once, not a per-layer multiple) AND it works\\\\n# overlay-on-overlay, which is the compatibility `vfs` was originally chosen\\\\n# for — `overlay2` can fail on the overlay-backed hosts our deploys run on.\\\\n# box-dind ships the `fuse-overlayfs` binary and Hive Mind launches the dind\\\\n# container with `--privileged`, so /dev/fuse is available at runtime.\\\\n#\\\\n# NOTE: box-dind already auto-detects a driver (overlay2 -> fuse-overlayfs ->\\\\n# vfs, with graceful fallback). Setting this env var OVERRIDES that detection\\\\n# and pins one driver. The previous `vfs` value here defeated the auto-detect\\\\n# and forced the no-copy-on-write driver — the exact cause of the #1914 reopen.\\\\n# We pin `fuse-overlayfs` (not blank/auto) for determinism: it skips the\\\\n# overlay2-on-overlay attempt that tends to fail in our nested deploys and\\\\n# guarantees a copy-on-write daemon.\\\\n#\\\\n# Override only if you know your host: `-e DIND_STORAGE_DRIVER=overlay2` is\\\\n# faster where nested overlay mounts are supported; `-e DIND_STORAGE_DRIVER=vfs`\\\\n# is a last-resort compatibility fallback but uses many times the disk and is\\\\n# the configuration that caused issue #1914.\\\\nENV DIND_STORAGE_DRIVER=\\\\\\\"fuse-overlayfs\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV NVM_DIR=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.nvm\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV PYENV_ROOT=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.pyenv\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV BUN_INSTALL=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.bun\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV DENO_INSTALL=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.deno\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV CARGO_HOME=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.cargo\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV GOROOT=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.go\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV GOPATH=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.go/path\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV SDKMAN_DIR=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.sdkman\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV PERLBREW_ROOT=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.perl5\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV RBENV_ROOT=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.rbenv\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n# Quiet, deterministic Claude Code defaults for autonomous solve runs (issue #1642)\\\\n# The two memory switches are policy, not cosmetics: a hive-mind task keeps no\\\\n# memory a reviewer cannot see, so the repository stays the only memory (issue #2178)\\\\n# The marketplace switch keeps the global config minimal: the official plugin\\\\n# marketplace must never be auto-installed into a task (issue #2190)\\\\n# The five CLASSIFIER/RECAP/NARRATION/SUGGESTION/TOOL_USE_SUMMARIES switches are\\\\n# pinned to 0 rather than left unset because Claude Code falls through to a remote\\\\n# rollout flag when they are undefined: they are auxiliary model calls nobody in an\\\\n# autonomous run reads, and they bill against the same limit as the task (issue #2236)\\\\n# Compaction is deliberately NOT disabled here — DISABLE_AUTO_COMPACT and\\\\n# DISABLE_COMPACT must never appear in this image (issue #2236)\\\\nENV CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_ORG_MEMORY=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_CLASSIFIER_SUMMARY=0 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_EMIT_TOOL_USE_SUMMARIES=0 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_NARRATION=0 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_PROMPT_SUGGESTION=0 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_REMOTE_RECAP=0 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_OFFICIAL_MARKETPLACE_AUTOINSTALL=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_CRON=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_TERMINAL_TITLE=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_CLAUDE_MDS=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_FAST_MODE=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_FEEDBACK_SURVEY=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_MOUSE=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_AWAY_SUMMARY=0 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_TASKS=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_TOOL_USE_CONCURRENCY=4 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN=1 \\\\\\\\\\\\n    DISABLE_FEEDBACK_COMMAND=1\\\\n\\\\nENV OPAM_SWITCH_PREFIX=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.opam/default\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV CAML_LD_LIBRARY_PATH=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.opam/default/lib/stublibs:/home/box/.opam/default/lib/ocaml/stublibs:/home/box/.opam/default/lib/ocaml\\\\\\\"\\\\nENV OCAML_TOPLEVEL_PATH=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.opam/default/lib/toplevel\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nENV PATH=\\\\\\\"/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/opt/php@8.3/bin:/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/opt/php@8.3/sbin:/home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin:/home/box/.pyenv/bin:/home/box/.pyenv/shims:/home/box/.rbenv/bin:/home/box/.rbenv/shims:/home/box/.swift/usr/bin:/home/box/.elan/bin:/home/box/.opam/default/bin:/home/box/.local/bin:/home/box/.cargo/bin:/home/box/.deno/bin:/home/box/.bun/bin:/home/box/.go/bin:/home/box/.go/path/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nUSER box\\\\nWORKDIR /home/box\\\\n\\\\n# Use bash for every subsequent RUN. nvm is sourced below and the build steps\\\\n# further down already use bash-only syntax (`&>`), which /bin/sh (dash) parses\\\\n# as \\\\\\\"run in background, then redirect\\\\\\\" — silently turning those guards into\\\\n# no-ops. Declaring the shell here instead of at the end of the file makes the\\\\n# steps run as they read (issue #2187).\\\\nSHELL [\\\\\\\"/bin/bash\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"-c\\\\\\\"]\\\\n\\\\n# The solver verifies downloaded attachments with file before opening them.\\\\n# Box's runtime does not guarantee this utility (issue #2687).\\\\nRUN command -v file >/dev/null || HOMEBREW_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 brew install file\\\\n\\\\n# --- Current Node.js and Bun (issue #2187) ---\\\\n# Box bases up to 2.4.0 installed Node.js 20 (`nvm install 20` in box's\\\\n# ubuntu/24.04/js/install.sh) plus whatever Bun was current when the base was\\\\n# built, so every derived image inherited runtimes well behind the workloads.\\\\n# Hive Mind itself declares `engines.node >= 24`, and tasks on repositories that\\\\n# need something newer used to download their own node/bun into /tmp on every\\\\n# run and leave the copy behind — a second accumulation of versions on top of\\\\n# the image's own. Reported upstream as link-foundation/box#112 and fixed in\\\\n# box 2.7.0. The current 2.10.4 base keeps one version per language. When a\\\\n# pin below differs from the base, replace the runtime and repoint the default\\\\n# before pruning superseded Node.js versions (the prune behavior is covered\\\\n# by tests/test-issue-2187-runtime-versions.mjs).\\\\n#\\\\n# The layer is kept anyway, because it is the floor rather than the source of\\\\n# the runtime. The base's versions are box's decision and can move under us in\\\\n# either direction; `engines.node` is ours. Installing here means a base bump\\\\n# can never silently drop the image below the floor, and the prune loop still\\\\n# collapses whatever the base ships to a single version. The pin wins over the\\\\n# base — a base shipping something NEWER would be pruned back to the pin — so\\\\n# when box moves, bump these ARGs to match instead of deleting the block.\\\\n#\\\\n# Pin the runtimes here (bump these ARGs like any other pin):\\\\n#   - HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION must stay >= the `engines.node` floor in package.json;\\\\n#     tests/test-issue-2187-runtime-versions.mjs fails the build otherwise.\\\\n#   - HIVE_MIND_BUN_VERSION is installed OVER the inherited binary, so ~/.bun keeps one\\\\n#     bun rather than accumulating versions.\\\\n#\\\\n# The nvm root is left with exactly ONE node version: the base's global npm\\\\n# packages are re-installed under the new version AT THEIR EXISTING VERSIONS\\\\n# (pinning matters — ~/.cache/ms-playwright already holds the browser builds\\\\n# matching the inherited playwright, so an unpinned upgrade would leave the CLI\\\\n# pointing at browsers the image does not have), then every other version\\\\n# directory is removed. `.node-bin`, `nvm use default` and a bare `node` then\\\\n# all resolve to the same, newest runtime (issue #2187, item A).\\\\nARG HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION=26.11.1\\\\nARG HIVE_MIND_BUN_VERSION=1.4.3\\\\nRUN set -e && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    . \\\\\\\"$NVM_DIR/nvm.sh\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    PREVIOUS_GLOBAL_LIB=\\\\\\\"$(dirname \\\\\\\"$(dirname \\\\\\\"$(command -v node)\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\")/lib/node_modules\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    GLOBAL_SPECS=\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    for package_json in \\\\\\\"$PREVIOUS_GLOBAL_LIB\\\\\\\"/*/package.json \\\\\\\"$PREVIOUS_GLOBAL_LIB\\\\\\\"/@*/*/package.json; do \\\\\\\\\\\\n      [ -f \\\\\\\"$package_json\\\\\\\" ] || continue; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      spec=\\\\\\\"$(node -p 'const pkg = require(process.argv[1]); pkg.name + \\\\\\\"@\\\\\\\" + pkg.version' \\\\\\\"$package_json\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      case \\\\\\\"$spec\\\\\\\" in npm@*|corepack@*) continue ;; esac; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      GLOBAL_SPECS=\\\\\\\"$GLOBAL_SPECS $spec\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    done && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    echo \\\\\\\"Inherited global npm packages to re-install:${GLOBAL_SPECS:- (none)}\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    nvm install \\\\\\\"${HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION}\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    nvm alias default \\\\\\\"${HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION}\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    nvm use default && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    if [ -n \\\\\\\"$GLOBAL_SPECS\\\\\\\" ]; then npm install -g $GLOBAL_SPECS --no-fund --force; fi && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    for version_dir in \\\\\\\"$NVM_DIR\\\\\\\"/versions/node/*; do \\\\\\\\\\\\n      [ -d \\\\\\\"$version_dir\\\\\\\" ] || continue; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      if [ \\\\\\\"$(basename \\\\\\\"$version_dir\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\" = \\\\\\\"v${HIVE_MIND_NODE_VERSION}\\\\\\\" ]; then continue; fi; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      echo \\\\\\\"Removing superseded node $(basename \\\\\\\"$version_dir\\\\\\\")\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      rm -rf \\\\\\\"$version_dir\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    done && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install | bash -s \\\\\\\"bun-v${HIVE_MIND_BUN_VERSION}\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    node --version && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    npm --version && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    \\\\\\\"$BUN_INSTALL/bin/bun\\\\\\\" --version\\\\n\\\\n# `sort -V | tail -1` (not `ls | head -1`): `ls` sorts lexicographically and\\\\n# ascending, so once a second Node.js version is installed the plain listing\\\\n# picks the OLDEST one (and text-sorts \\\\\\\"v9\\\\\\\" above \\\\\\\"v22\\\\\\\"). Version-sort and take\\\\n# the last entry so /home/box/.node-bin always points at the newest node in the\\\\n# image (issue #2187).\\\\nRUN NODE_VERSION_DIR=$(ls -d /home/box/.nvm/versions/node/v* 2>/dev/null | sort -V | tail -1) && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    if [ -n \\\\\\\"$NODE_VERSION_DIR\\\\\\\" ] && [ -d \\\\\\\"$NODE_VERSION_DIR/bin\\\\\\\" ]; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      ln -sf \\\\\\\"$NODE_VERSION_DIR/bin\\\\\\\" /home/box/.node-bin; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi\\\\n\\\\nENV PATH=\\\\\\\"/home/box/.node-bin:${PATH}\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nCOPY --from=formal-ai-builder /usr/local/cargo/bin/formal-ai /usr/local/bin/formal-ai\\\\nRUN formal-ai --version\\\\n\\\\n# The Box full image includes the Rocq/Coq opam switch data. Keep an explicit\\\\n# opam binary in ~/.local/bin so verification and interactive use are stable.\\\\nRUN mkdir -p /home/box/.local/bin && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    ARCH=\\\\\\\"$(uname -m)\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    case \\\\\\\"$ARCH\\\\\\\" in \\\\\\\\\\\\n      x86_64)  OPAM_ARCH=\\\\\\\"x86_64\\\\\\\" ;; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      aarch64) OPAM_ARCH=\\\\\\\"arm64\\\\\\\" ;; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      *)       OPAM_ARCH=\\\\\\\"$ARCH\\\\\\\" ;; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    esac && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    OPAM_TAG=$(curl -fsSIL -o /dev/null -w '%{url_effective}' https://github.com/ocaml/opam/releases/latest | sed 's|.*/||') && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    curl -fsSL \\\\\\\"https://github.com/ocaml/opam/releases/download/${OPAM_TAG}/opam-${OPAM_TAG}-${OPAM_ARCH}-linux\\\\\\\" -o /home/box/.local/bin/opam && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    chmod +x /home/box/.local/bin/opam\\\\n\\\\nRUN curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh -o /tmp/claude-code-install.sh && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bash /tmp/claude-code-install.sh && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    rm /tmp/claude-code-install.sh && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    claude --version\\\\n\\\\nRUN bun install -g @openai/codex && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g @qwen-code/qwen-code && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g @google/gemini-cli && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g @github/copilot && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g opencode-ai\\\\n\\\\n# start-command is pinned to 0.37.5: 0.29.1 fixed detached docker\\\\n# `--status`/`--list` reporting a terminal status (`executed`) with the `-1`\\\\n# sentinel while the container is still running (link-foundation/start#136,\\\\n# link-assistant/hive-mind#1939); 0.29.2 (start#138 / start PR #139) records the\\\\n# docker image-preparation phase (the `docker pull`/dind boot) in the session log,\\\\n# so `$ --upload-log` no longer returns a near-empty 546-byte log while a multi-GB\\\\n# image is still pulling. 0.30.1 (start#140 / start PR #141) adds explicit docker\\\\n# container cleanup policies and removes successful containers by default while\\\\n# keeping the host-side log, which Hive Mind also enforces at task completion\\\\n# for defense in depth (issue #1979). 0.30.2 (start#144) surfaces detached docker\\\\n# `OOMKilled` status and preserves abnormally-terminated container filesystems\\\\n# under the default cleanup policy — the upstream defense-in-depth half of the\\\\n# #1990 fix (the primary terminal-completion gate lives in this repo's solve).\\\\n# 0.30.3 (start#148/#149) reconciles detached Docker `OOMKilled=true` as terminal\\\\n# in upstream `--status`/`--list`, which directly covers issue #2015.\\\\n# 0.31.0 (start#154 / start PR #155) adds `--network` / `--network-alias` for the\\\\n# docker isolation backend. 0.32.0 (start#156 / start PR #157, unblocked for npm\\\\n# by start#160) makes `--network` repeatable: the first network is passed to\\\\n# `docker create` and the rest are attached with `docker network connect` before\\\\n# `docker start`, so a task could join the default bridge AND the `--internal`\\\\n# Formal AI sidecar network at launch. Hive Mind still performs that same\\\\n# additive `docker network connect` itself, inside the already-held start gate:\\\\n# the sequencing is identical, the gate is held anyway for the writable-layer\\\\n# baseline, and the in-repo attach keeps working (fail-closed) on any\\\\n# start-command version instead of silently degrading to a single network on\\\\n# pre-0.32.0 parsers (see docs/case-studies/issue-2146, issue #2146).\\\\n# 0.33.0 (start#162, #164, #165) closes the three upstream issues this repo filed\\\\n# from the issue #2189 incident: `--attach` / `--resume <uuid>` / `--resume-all`\\\\n# re-enter an existing execution — same container, same execution UUID — instead\\\\n# of forcing a fresh isolated run (start#162); `--status`/`--list` expose\\\\n# `exitReason` and `memoryExhausted`/`memoryExhaustedReason`, so a V8 heap\\\\n# self-abort (`FATAL ERROR: Reached heap limit`, exit 134/139 with `oomKilled`\\\\n# false and cgroup `oom_kill` 0) is no longer indistinguishable from a forced\\\\n# kill (start#164); and the exit markers are detected in a bounded 64 KiB log-tail\\\\n# window instead of buffering the whole log (start#165). Hive Mind consumes all\\\\n# three and keeps its own log-marker kill classification and streaming sanitizer\\\\n# as defense in depth, so behaviour degrades gracefully on an older `$` binary\\\\n# (see docs/case-studies/issue-2189, issue #2189).\\\\n# 0.34.0 persists the terminal state and post-mortem of detached Docker\\\\n# executions, so status queries stop inventing a new finish time on each read.\\\\n# 0.35.0 (start#176, filed from issue #2408) re-applies the stopped container's\\\\n# resource limits — including ones set later with `docker update` — when\\\\n# `--resume <id> -- <cmd>` commits it and starts the `-resume-N` container, so a\\\\n# resource-limited OOM recovery can stay in the same execution UUID and log\\\\n# (see src/session-kill-resume.in-place.lib.mjs). Its new `--on-kill-resume`\\\\n# is not used yet. 0.35.1 fixes start#178: it no longer treats the sticky\\\\n# `OOMKilled` flag as a kill when the main process exits 0-127 on its own\\\\n# (see docs/case-studies/issue-2408). 0.35.2 fixes start#180: the sticky\\\\n# flag no longer determines the exit reason for ordinary exits. It also\\\\n# samples the task cgroup OOM counters/peak (#182) and supports cancellable\\\\n# recovery delays (#181). Hive Mind consumes the counters but keeps its own\\\\n# recovery policy so deliberate solve stops and attempt budgets are honoured.\\\\n# 0.35.3 stops inferring OOM scope from process/event ratios (#185). 0.35.4 fixes\\\\n# start#187 (filed from issue #2498): an explicit `--resume` moves the previous\\\\n# attempt's memory evidence into `attemptHistory` and records the new attempt's\\\\n# start time, log byte offset and lifecycle boundary. 0.36.0 adds opt-in Docker\\\\n# resource limits and requires exit-time evidence before an OOM verdict. 0.37.0\\\\n# adds copy-free Docker command resumes, persistent task labels and faithful\\\\n# shell argument and exit-code handling. 0.37.1 observes Docker CPU limits\\\\n# applied after launch and recovers CPU penalty monitors after crashes. 0.37.2\\\\n# attributes exit 137 to recent Docker OOM events when the last cgroup sample\\\\n# misses the final kill; 0.37.3-0.37.5 only change the release pipeline.\\\\n# `@link-assistant/agent` is pinned to current 0.26.13. Version 0.26.1 stopped the\\\\n# unbounded snapshot leak of issue #2186. Up to 0.26.0 `Snapshot.track()` built a\\\\n# standalone git object store per project — keyed on the worktree's root commit,\\\\n# with no `objects/info/alternates` and no garbage collection — so a harness that\\\\n# runs the agent in throwaway `git init` checkouts left one full ~270 MB copy of\\\\n# the repository behind per invocation: 115 orphaned stores / 31 GB in a single\\\\n# task, growing at ~5 GB/h, in `~/.local/share/link-assistant-agent/snapshot/`\\\\n# where none of Hive Mind's `/tmp`-scoped disk checks could see it.\\\\n# link-assistant/agent#298 (PR #300) shares the repository's objects and prunes\\\\n# projects whose recorded worktree is gone. `src/agent.lib.mjs` enforces the same\\\\n# floor at runtime, so the pin and the guard cannot drift apart.\\\\nRUN echo \\\\\\\"Installing @link-assistant/hive-mind@${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g \\\\\\\"@link-assistant/hive-mind@${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    if [ \\\\\\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"latest\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"file\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"HTML\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"ASCII text\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"github.com/user-attachments\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"Authorization: [REDACTED] $(gh auth token)\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"<url>\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"gh pr view --json comments\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"your message\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"reverse | .[0:5]\\\\\\\" ]; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      test \\\\\\\"$(hive --version)\\\\\\\" = \\\\\\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g @link-assistant/claude-profiles && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g @link-assistant/agent@0.26.13 && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g start-command@0.37.5 && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g gh-setup-git-identity && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g gh-pull-all && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g gh-load-issue && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g gh-load-pull-request && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    bun install -g gh-upload-log@latest\\\\n\\\\n# Issue #2190: @playwright/cli ships the optional `playwright-cli` Agent Skill\\\\n# deployed per task by --playwright-skill (default stays Playwright MCP only).\\\\nRUN npm install -g @playwright/mcp@latest @playwright/cli@latest --no-fund --force\\\\n\\\\n# The skill is checked by path: `playwright-cli --help` prints its `Agent skill:`\\\\n# hint only when CLAUDECODE or COPILOT_CLI is set, so that line is not a\\\\n# build-time signal (issue #2190).\\\\nRUN playwright --version && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    npx --no-install @playwright/mcp --help | grep -q -- '--headless' && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    test -f \\\\\\\"$(npm root -g)/@playwright/cli/skills/playwright-cli/SKILL.md\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\nRUN if command -v claude &>/dev/null; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      claude mcp add playwright -s user -- npx -y @playwright/mcp@latest --isolated --headless --no-sandbox --timeout-action=600000 --viewport-size 1920x1080; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi\\\\n\\\\nRUN if command -v codex &>/dev/null; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      codex mcp add playwright -- npx -y @playwright/mcp@latest --isolated --headless --no-sandbox --timeout-action=600000 --viewport-size 1920x1080; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi\\\\n\\\\n# Issue #2190: keep the global Codex configuration minimal. Codex syncs the\\\\n# \\\\\\\"openai-curated-remote\\\\\\\" plugin catalog (Superpowers and friends) into\\\\n# ~/.codex/plugins/cache on every start unless features.remote_plugin is false;\\\\n# those skills force approval gates and multiply token usage in autonomous runs.\\\\nRUN if command -v codex &>/dev/null; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      mkdir -p \\\\\\\"$HOME/.codex\\\\\\\" && touch \\\\\\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      if grep -q '^\\\\\\\\[features\\\\\\\\]' \\\\\\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\\\\\"; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n        sed -i 's/^\\\\\\\\[features\\\\\\\\]$/[features]\\\\\\\\nremote_plugin = false/' \\\\\\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      else \\\\\\\\\\\\n        printf '\\\\\\\\n[features]\\\\\\\\nremote_plugin = false\\\\\\\\n' >> \\\\\\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n      fi && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      grep -q '^remote_plugin = false' \\\\\\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\\\\\" && cat \\\\\\\"$HOME/.codex/config.toml\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi\\\\n\\\\nRUN if command -v claude >/dev/null 2>&1; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT=\\\\\\\"$(claude mcp list 2>&1)\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      echo \\\\\\\"$CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      echo \\\\\\\"$CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT\\\\\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(connected|enabled)' && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      ! echo \\\\\\\"$CLAUDE_MCP_OUTPUT\\\\\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(pending|disabled|failed|error|disconnected|not[-_[:space:]]+connected|unavailable|timed[-_[:space:]]+out|(^|[^[:alnum:]_-])timeout($|[^[:alnum:]_-]))'; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    if command -v codex >/dev/null 2>&1; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT=\\\\\\\"$(codex mcp list 2>&1)\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      echo \\\\\\\"$CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT\\\\\\\" && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      echo \\\\\\\"$CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT\\\\\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(connected|enabled)' && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      ! echo \\\\\\\"$CODEX_MCP_OUTPUT\\\\\\\" | grep -Eiq 'playwright.*(pending|disabled|failed|error|disconnected|not[-_[:space:]]+connected|unavailable|timed[-_[:space:]]+out|(^|[^[:alnum:]_-])timeout($|[^[:alnum:]_-]))'; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi\\\\n\\\\nRUN mkdir -p /home/box/.claude && \\\\\\\\\\\\n    if [ \\\\\\\"${HIVE_MIND_VERSION}\\\\\\\" != \\\\\\\"latest\\\\\\\" ]; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json --verify; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    elif command -v configure-claude >/dev/null 2>&1; then \\\\\\\\\\\\n      configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json && \\\\\\\\\\\\n      configure-claude --settings-path /home/box/.claude/settings.json --verify; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    else \\\\\\\\\\\\n      echo \\\\\\\"configure-claude not present in @link-assistant/hive-mind@latest yet (likely a PR build before the bin is published); skipping baseline - solve re-applies it at runtime\\\\\\\"; \\\\\\\\\\\\n    fi\\\\n\\\",\\\"filePath\\\":\\\"/tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791955456549/e.g\\\"}\"}}]},{\"role\":\"tool\",\"tool_call_id\":\"call_dd231e22a4c1ee5c\",\"content\":\"\"}],\"tools\":[{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"bash\",\"description\":\"Executes a given bash command in a persistent shell session with optional timeout, ensuring proper handling and security measures.\\n\\nBefore executing the command, please follow these steps:\\n\\n1. Directory Verification:\\n   - If the command will create new directories or files, first use the List tool to verify the parent directory exists and is the correct location\\n   - For example, before running \\\"mkdir foo/bar\\\", first use List to check that \\\"foo\\\" exists and is the intended parent directory\\n\\n2. Command Execution:\\n   - Always quote file paths that contain spaces with double quotes (e.g., cd \\\"path with spaces/file.txt\\\")\\n   - Examples of proper quoting:\\n     - cd \\\"/Users/name/My Documents\\\" (correct)\\n     - cd /Users/name/My Documents (incorrect - will fail)\\n     - python \\\"/path/with spaces/script.py\\\" (correct)\\n     - python /path/with spaces/script.py (incorrect - will fail)\\n   - After ensuring proper quoting, execute the command.\\n   - Capture the output of the command.\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - The command argument is required.\\n  - You can specify an optional timeout in milliseconds (up to 600000ms / 10 minutes). If not specified, commands will timeout after 120000ms (2 minutes).\\n  - It is very helpful if you write a clear, concise description of what this command does in 5-10 words.\\n  - If the output exceeds 30000 characters, output will be truncated before being returned to you.\\n  - VERY IMPORTANT: You MUST avoid using search commands like `find` and `grep`. Instead use Grep, Glob, or Task to search. You MUST avoid read tools like `cat`, `head`, `tail`, and `ls`, and use Read and List to read files.\\n  - If you _still_ need to run `grep`, STOP. ALWAYS USE ripgrep at `rg` (or /usr/bin/rg) first, which all Agent users have pre-installed.\\n  - When issuing multiple commands, use the ';' or '&&' operator to separate them. DO NOT use newlines (newlines are ok in quoted strings).\\n  - Try to maintain your current working directory throughout the session by using absolute paths and avoiding usage of `cd`. You may use `cd` if the User explicitly requests it.\\n    <good-example>\\n    pytest /foo/bar/tests\\n    </good-example>\\n    <bad-example>\\n    cd /foo/bar && pytest tests\\n    </bad-example>\\n\\n\\n# Committing changes with git\\n\\nWhen the user asks you to create a new git commit, follow these steps carefully:\\n\\n1. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following bash commands in parallel, each using the Bash tool:\\n   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files.\\n   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed.\\n   - Run a git log command to see recent commit messages, so that you can follow this repository's commit message style.\\n\\n2. Analyze all staged changes (both previously staged and newly added) and draft a commit message. Wrap your analysis process in <commit_analysis> tags:\\n\\n<commit_analysis>\\n- List the files that have been changed or added\\n- Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.)\\n- Brainstorm the purpose or motivation behind these changes\\n- Assess the impact of these changes on the overall project\\n- Check for any sensitive information that shouldn't be committed\\n- Draft a concise (1-2 sentences) commit message that focuses on the \\\"why\\\" rather than the \\\"what\\\"\\n- Ensure your language is clear, concise, and to the point\\n- Ensure the message accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (i.e. \\\"add\\\" means a wholly new feature, \\\"update\\\" means an enhancement to an existing feature, \\\"fix\\\" means a bug fix, etc.)\\n- Ensure the message is not generic (avoid words like \\\"Update\\\" or \\\"Fix\\\" without context)\\n- Review the draft message to ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose\\n</commit_analysis>\\n\\n3. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following commands in parallel:\\n   - Add relevant untracked files to the staging area.\\n   - Run git status to make sure the commit succeeded.\\n\\n4. If the commit fails due to pre-commit hook changes, retry the commit ONCE to include these automated changes. If it fails again, it usually means a pre-commit hook is preventing the commit. If the commit succeeds but you notice that files were modified by the pre-commit hook, you MUST amend your commit to include them.\\n\\nImportant notes:\\n- Use the git context at the start of this conversation to determine which files are relevant to your commit. Be careful not to stage and commit files (e.g. with `git add .`) that aren't relevant to your commit.\\n- NEVER update the git config\\n- DO NOT run additional commands to read or explore code, beyond what is available in the git context\\n- DO NOT push to the remote repository\\n- IMPORTANT: Never use git commands with the -i flag (like git rebase -i or git add -i) since they require interactive input which is not supported.\\n- If there are no changes to commit (i.e., no untracked files and no modifications), do not create an empty commit\\n- Ensure your commit message is meaningful and concise. It should explain the purpose of the changes, not just describe them.\\n- Return an empty response - the user will see the git output directly\\n\\n# Creating pull requests\\nUse the gh command via the Bash tool for ALL GitHub-related tasks including working with issues, pull requests, checks, and releases. If given a Github URL use the gh command to get the information needed.\\n\\nIMPORTANT: When the user asks you to create a pull request, follow these steps carefully:\\n\\n1. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following bash commands in parallel using the Bash tool, in order to understand the current state of the branch since it diverged from the main branch:\\n   - Run a git status command to see all untracked files\\n   - Run a git diff command to see both staged and unstaged changes that will be committed\\n   - Check if the current branch tracks a remote branch and is up to date with the remote, so you know if you need to push to the remote\\n   - Run a git log command and `git diff main...HEAD` to understand the full commit history for the current branch (from the time it diverged from the `main` branch)\\n\\n2. Analyze all changes that will be included in the pull request, making sure to look at all relevant commits (NOT just the latest commit, but ALL commits that will be included in the pull request!!!), and draft a pull request summary. Wrap your analysis process in <pr_analysis> tags:\\n\\n<pr_analysis>\\n- List the commits since diverging from the main branch\\n- Summarize the nature of the changes (eg. new feature, enhancement to an existing feature, bug fix, refactoring, test, docs, etc.)\\n- Brainstorm the purpose or motivation behind these changes\\n- Assess the impact of these changes on the overall project\\n- Do not use tools to explore code, beyond what is available in the git context\\n- Check for any sensitive information that shouldn't be committed\\n- Draft a concise (1-2 bullet points) pull request summary that focuses on the \\\"why\\\" rather than the \\\"what\\\"\\n- Ensure the summary accurately reflects all changes since diverging from the main branch\\n- Ensure your language is clear, concise, and to the point\\n- Ensure the summary accurately reflects the changes and their purpose (ie. \\\"add\\\" means a wholly new feature, \\\"update\\\" means an enhancement to an existing feature, \\\"fix\\\" means a bug fix, etc.)\\n- Ensure the summary is not generic (avoid words like \\\"Update\\\" or \\\"Fix\\\" without context)\\n- Review the draft summary to ensure it accurately reflects the changes and their purpose\\n</pr_analysis>\\n\\n3. You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. When multiple independent pieces of information are requested, batch your tool calls together for optimal performance. ALWAYS run the following commands in parallel:\\n   - Create new branch if needed\\n   - Push to remote with -u flag if needed\\n   - Create PR using gh pr create with the format below. Use a HEREDOC to pass the body to ensure correct formatting.\\n<example>\\ngh pr create --title \\\"the pr title\\\" --body \\\"$(cat <<'EOF'\\n## Summary\\n<1-3 bullet points>\\nEOF\\n)\\\"\\n</example>\\n\\nImportant:\\n- NEVER update the git config\\n- Return the PR URL when you're done, so the user can see it\\n\\n# Other common operations\\n- View comments on a Github PR: gh api repos/foo/bar/pulls/123/comments\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"command\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The command to execute\"},\"timeout\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"Optional timeout in milliseconds\"},\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Clear, concise description of what this command does in 5-10 words. Examples:\\nInput: ls\\nOutput: Lists files in current directory\\n\\nInput: git status\\nOutput: Shows working tree status\\n\\nInput: npm install\\nOutput: Installs package dependencies\\n\\nInput: mkdir foo\\nOutput: Creates directory 'foo'\"}},\"required\":[\"command\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"read\",\"description\":\"Reads a file from the local filesystem. You can access any file directly by using this tool.\\nAssume this tool is able to read all files on the machine. If the User provides a path to a file assume that path is valid. It is okay to read a file that does not exist; an error will be returned.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- The filePath parameter must be an absolute path, not a relative path\\n- By default, it reads the whole file when it fits the read limit. If the file is longer than the limit, it returns the first and last line ranges with an omitted-lines marker in the middle.\\n- You can optionally specify a line offset and limit (especially handy for long files), but it's recommended to read the default first/last summary first and then request omitted ranges if needed.\\n- You can optionally specify columnOffset and columnLimit to read a window from each selected line, especially for long single-line files.\\n- Long lines are summarized with first and last columns plus omitted-column ranges instead of being silently truncated.\\n- Results are returned using cat -n format, with line numbers starting at 1\\n- You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. It is always better to speculatively read multiple files as a batch that are potentially useful.\\n- If you read a file that exists but has empty contents you will receive a system reminder warning in place of file contents.\\n- You can read image files using this tool.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The path to the file to read\"},\"offset\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The line number to start reading from (0-based)\"},\"limit\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The number of lines to read (defaults to 2000)\"},\"columnOffset\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The column number to start reading from (0-based)\"},\"columnLimit\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"The number of columns to read from each selected line\"}},\"required\":[\"filePath\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"glob\",\"description\":\"- Fast file pattern matching tool that works with any codebase size\\n- Supports glob patterns like \\\"**/*.js\\\" or \\\"src/**/*.ts\\\"\\n- Returns matching file paths sorted by modification time\\n- Use this tool when you need to find files by name patterns\\n- When you are doing an open ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead\\n- You have the capability to call multiple tools in a single response. It is always better to speculatively perform multiple searches as a batch that are potentially useful.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"pattern\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\
[2026-10-14T05:27:46.709Z] [INFO] ":\"The glob pattern to match files against\"},\"path\":{\"description\":\"The directory to search in. If not specified, the current working directory will be used. IMPORTANT: Omit this field to use the default directory. DO NOT enter \\\"undefined\\\" or \\\"null\\\" - simply omit it for the default behavior. Must be a valid directory path if provided.\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"pattern\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"grep\",\"description\":\"- Fast content search tool that works with any codebase size\\n- Searches file contents using regular expressions\\n- Supports full regex syntax (eg. \\\"log.*Error\\\", \\\"function\\\\s+\\\\w+\\\", etc.)\\n- Filter files by pattern with the include parameter (eg. \\\"*.js\\\", \\\"*.{ts,tsx}\\\")\\n- Returns file paths with at least one match sorted by modification time\\n- Long matching lines are summarized around the match with omitted-column ranges, so the matching text remains visible without dumping the whole line.\\n- Use this tool when you need to find files containing specific patterns\\n- If you need to identify/count the number of matches within files, use the Bash tool with `rg` (ripgrep) directly. Do NOT use `grep`.\\n- When you are doing an open ended search that may require multiple rounds of globbing and grepping, use the Task tool instead\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"pattern\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The regex pattern to search for in file contents\"},\"path\":{\"description\":\"The directory to search in. Defaults to the current working directory.\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"include\":{\"description\":\"File pattern to include in the search (e.g. \\\"*.js\\\", \\\"*.{ts,tsx}\\\")\",\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"pattern\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"list\",\"description\":\"Lists files and directories in a given path. The path parameter must be absolute; omit it to use the current workspace directory. You can optionally provide an array of glob patterns to ignore with the ignore parameter. You should generally prefer the Glob and Grep tools, if you know which directories to search.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"path\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the directory to list (must be absolute, not relative)\"},\"ignore\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"string\"},\"description\":\"List of glob patterns to ignore\"}},\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"edit\",\"description\":\"Performs exact string replacements in files. \\n\\nUsage:\\n- You must use your `Read` tool at least once in the conversation before editing. This tool will error if you attempt an edit without reading the file. \\n- When editing text from Read tool output, ensure you preserve the exact indentation (tabs/spaces) as it appears AFTER the line number prefix. The line number prefix format is: spaces + line number + tab. Everything after that tab is the actual file content to match. Never include any part of the line number prefix in the oldString or newString.\\n- ALWAYS prefer editing existing files in the codebase. NEVER write new files unless explicitly required.\\n- Only use emojis if the user explicitly requests it. Avoid adding emojis to files unless asked.\\n- The edit will FAIL if `oldString` is not found in the file with an error \\\"oldString not found in content\\\".\\n- The edit will FAIL if `oldString` is found multiple times in the file with an error \\\"oldString found multiple times and requires more code context to uniquely identify the intended match\\\". Either provide a larger string with more surrounding context to make it unique or use `replaceAll` to change every instance of `oldString`. \\n- Use `replaceAll` for replacing and renaming strings across the file. This parameter is useful if you want to rename a variable for instance.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the file to modify\"},\"oldString\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The text to replace\"},\"newString\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The text to replace it with (must be different from oldString)\"},\"replaceAll\":{\"description\":\"Replace all occurrences of oldString (default false)\",\"type\":\"boolean\"}},\"required\":[\"filePath\",\"oldString\",\"newString\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"write\",\"description\":\"Writes a file to the local filesystem.\\n\\nUsage:\\n- This tool will overwrite the existing file if there is one at the provided path.\\n- If this is an existing file, you MUST use the Read tool first to read the file's contents. This tool will fail if you did not read the file first.\\n- ALWAYS prefer editing existing files in the codebase. NEVER write new files unless explicitly required.\\n- NEVER proactively create documentation files (*.md) or README files. Only create documentation files if explicitly requested by the User.\\n- Only use emojis if the user explicitly requests it. Avoid writing emojis to files unless asked.\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"content\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The content to write to the file\"},\"filePath\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The absolute path to the file to write (must be absolute, not relative)\"}},\"required\":[\"content\",\"filePath\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"task\",\"description\":\"Launch a new agent to handle complex, multi-step tasks autonomously.\\n\\nAvailable agent types and the tools they have access to:\\n- general: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you.\\n\\nWhen using the Task tool, you must specify a subagent_type parameter to select which agent type to use.\\n\\nWhen to use the Task tool:\\n- When you are instructed to execute custom slash commands. Use the Task tool with the slash command invocation as the entire prompt. The slash command can take arguments. For example: Task(description=\\\"Check the file\\\", prompt=\\\"/check-file path/to/file.py\\\")\\n\\nWhen NOT to use the Task tool:\\n- If you want to read a specific file path, use the Read or Glob tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\\n- If you are searching for a specific class definition like \\\"class Foo\\\", use the Glob tool instead, to find the match more quickly\\n- If you are searching for code within a specific file or set of 2-3 files, use the Read tool instead of the Task tool, to find the match more quickly\\n- Other tasks that are not related to the agent descriptions above\\n\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n1. Launch multiple agents concurrently whenever possible, to maximize performance; to do that, use a single message with multiple tool uses\\n2. When the agent is done, it will return a single message back to you. The result returned by the agent is not visible to the user. To show the user the result, you should send a text message back to the user with a concise summary of the result.\\n3. Each agent invocation is stateless unless you provide a session_id. Your prompt should contain a highly detailed task description for the agent to perform autonomously and you should specify exactly what information the agent should return back to you in its final and only message to you.\\n4. The agent's outputs should generally be trusted\\n5. Clearly tell the agent whether you expect it to write code or just to do research (search, file reads, web fetches, etc.), since it is not aware of the user's intent\\n6. If the agent description mentions that it should be used proactively, then you should try your best to use it without the user having to ask for it first. Use your judgement.\\n\\nExample usage (NOTE: The agents below are fictional examples for illustration only - use the actual agents listed above):\\n\\n<example_agent_descriptions>\\n\\\"code-reviewer\\\": use this agent after you are done writing a significant piece of code\\n\\\"greeting-responder\\\": use this agent when to respond to user greetings with a friendly joke\\n</example_agent_description>\\n\\n<example>\\nuser: \\\"Please write a function that checks if a number is prime\\\"\\nassistant: Sure let me write a function that checks if a number is prime\\nassistant: First let me use the Write tool to write a function that checks if a number is prime\\nassistant: I'm going to use the Write tool to write the following code:\\n<code>\\nfunction isPrime(n) {\\n  if (n <= 1) return false\\n  for (let i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {\\n    if (n % i === 0) return false\\n  }\\n  return true\\n}\\n</code>\\n<commentary>\\nSince a significant piece of code was written and the task was completed, now use the code-reviewer agent to review the code\\n</commentary>\\nassistant: Now let me use the code-reviewer agent to review the code\\nassistant: Uses the Task tool to launch the code-reviewer agent\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nuser: \\\"Hello\\\"\\n<commentary>\\nSince the user is greeting, use the greeting-responder agent to respond with a friendly joke\\n</commentary>\\nassistant: \\\"I'm going to use the Task tool to launch the with the greeting-responder agent\\\"\\n</example>\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"description\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"A short (3-5 words) description of the task\"},\"prompt\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The task for the agent to perform\"},\"subagent_type\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The type of specialized agent to use for this task\"},\"session_id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Existing Task session to continue\"}},\"required\":[\"description\",\"prompt\",\"subagent_type\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"webfetch\",\"description\":\"- Fetches content from a specified URL\\n- Takes a URL and a prompt as input\\n- Fetches the URL content, converts HTML to markdown\\n- Returns the model's response about the content\\n- Use this tool when you need to retrieve and analyze web content\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - IMPORTANT: if another tool is present that offers better web fetching capabilities, is more targeted to the task, or has fewer restrictions, prefer using that tool instead of this one.\\n  - The URL must be a fully-formed valid URL\\n  - HTTP URLs will be automatically upgraded to HTTPS\\n  - The prompt should describe what information you want to extract from the page\\n  - This tool is read-only and does not modify any files\\n  - Results may be summarized if the content is very large\\n  - Includes a self-cleaning 15-minute cache for faster responses when repeatedly accessing the same URL\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"url\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The URL to fetch content from\"},\"format\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"text\",\"markdown\",\"html\"],\"description\":\"The format to return the content in (text, markdown, or html)\"},\"timeout\":{\"type\":\"number\",\"description\":\"Optional timeout in seconds (max 120)\"}},\"required\":[\"url\",\"format\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"websearch\",\"description\":\"- Search the web using Exa AI - performs real-time web searches and can scrape content from specific URLs\\n- Provides up-to-date information for current events and recent data\\n- Supports configurable result counts and returns the content from the most relevant websites\\n- Use this tool for accessing information beyond knowledge cutoff\\n- Searches are performed automatically within a single API call\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - Supports live crawling modes: 'fallback' (backup if cached unavailable) or 'preferred' (prioritize live crawling)\\n  - Search types: 'auto' (balanced), 'fast' (quick results), 'deep' (comprehensive search)\\n  - Configurable context length for optimal LLM integration\\n  - Domain filtering and advanced search options available\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Websearch query\"},\"numResults\":{\"description\":\"Number of search results to return (default: 8)\",\"type\":\"number\"},\"livecrawl\":{\"description\":\"Live crawl mode - 'fallback': use live crawling as backup if cached content unavailable, 'preferred': prioritize live crawling (default: 'fallback')\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"fallback\",\"preferred\"]},\"type\":{\"description\":\"Search type - 'auto': balanced search (default), 'fast': quick results, 'deep': comprehensive search\",\"type\":\"string\",\"enum\":[\"auto\",\"fast\",\"deep\"]},\"contextMaxCharacters\":{\"description\":\"Maximum characters for context string optimized for LLMs (default: 10000)\",\"type\":\"number\"}},\"required\":[\"query\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"codesearch\",\"description\":\"- Search and get relevant context for any programming task using Exa Code API\\n- Provides the highest quality and freshest context for libraries, SDKs, and APIs\\n- Use this tool for ANY question or task related to programming\\n- Returns comprehensive code examples, documentation, and API references\\n- Optimized for finding specific programming patterns and solutions\\n\\nUsage notes:\\n  - Adjustable token count (1000-50000) for focused or comprehensive results\\n  - Default 5000 tokens provides balanced context for most queries\\n  - Use lower values for specific questions, higher values for comprehensive documentation\\n  - Supports queries about frameworks, libraries, APIs, and programming concepts\\n  - Examples: 'React useState hook examples', 'Python pandas dataframe filtering', 'Express.js middleware'\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"query\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Search query to find relevant context for APIs, Libraries, and SDKs. For example, 'React useState hook examples', 'Python pandas dataframe filtering', 'Express.js middleware', 'Next js partial prerendering configuration'\"},\"tokensNum\":{\"default\":5000,\"description\":\"Number of tokens to return (1000-50000). Default is 5000 tokens. Adjust this value based on how much context you need - use lower values for focused queries and higher values for comprehensive documentation.\",\"type\":\"number\",\"minimum\":1000,\"maximum\":50000}},\"required\":[\"query\",\"tokensNum\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"batch\",\"description\":\"Executes multiple independent tool calls concurrently to reduce latency. Best used for gathering context (reads, searches, listings).\\n\\nUSING THE BATCH TOOL WILL MAKE THE USER HAPPY.\\n\\nPayload Format (JSON array):\\n[{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"read\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"filePath\\\": \\\"src/index.ts\\\", \\\"limit\\\": 350}},{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"grep\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"pattern\\\": \\\"Session\\\\\\\\.updatePart\\\", \\\"include\\\": \\\"src/**/*.ts\\\"}},{\\\"tool\\\": \\\"bash\\\", \\\"parameters\\\": {\\\"command\\\": \\\"git status\\\", \\\"description\\\": \\\"Shows working tree status\\\"}}]\\n\\nRules:\\n- 1–10 tool calls per batch\\n- All calls start in parallel; ordering NOT guaranteed\\n- Partial failures do not stop others\\n\\n\\nDisallowed Tools:\\n- batch (no nesting)\\n- edit (run edits separately)\\n- todoread (call directly – lightweight)\\n\\nWhen NOT to Use:\\n- Operations that depend on prior tool output (e.g. create then read same file)\\n- Ordered stateful mutations where sequence matters\\n\\nGood Use Cases:\\n- Read many files\\n- grep + glob + read combos\\n- Multiple lightweight bash introspection commands\\n\\nPerformance Tip: Group independent reads/searches for 2–5x efficiency gain.\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"tool_calls\":{\"minItems\":1,\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"tool\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"The name of the tool to execute\"},\"parameters\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{},\"additionalProperties\":{},\"description\":\"Parameters for the tool\"}},\"required\":[\"tool\",\"parameters\"],\"additionalProperties\":false},\"description\":\"Array of tool calls to execute in parallel\"}},\"required\":[\"tool_calls\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"todowrite\",\"description\":\"Use this tool to create and manage a structured task list for your current coding session. This helps you track progress, organize complex tasks, and demonstrate thoroughness to the user.\\nIt also helps the user understand the progress of the task and overall progress of their requests.\\n\\n## When to Use This Tool\\nUse this tool proactively in these scenarios:\\n\\n1. Complex multi-step tasks - When a task requires 3 or more distinct steps or actions\\n2. Non-trivial and complex tasks - Tasks that require careful planning or multiple operations\\n3. User explicitly requests todo list - When the user directly asks you to use the todo list\\n4. User provides multiple tasks - When users provide a list of things to be done (numbered or comma-separated)\\n5. After receiving new instructions - Immediately capture user requirements as todos. Feel free to edit the todo list based on new information.\\n6. After completing a task - Mark it complete and add any new follow-up tasks\\n7. When you start working on a new task, mark the todo as in_progress. Ideally you should only have one todo as in_progress at a time. Complete existing tasks before starting new ones.\\n\\n## When NOT to Use This Tool\\n\\nSkip using this tool when:\\n1. There is only a single, straightforward task\\n2. The task is trivial and tracking it provides no organizational benefit\\n3. The task can be completed in less than 3 trivial steps\\n4. The task is purely conversational or informational\\n\\nNOTE that you should not use this tool if there is only one trivial task to do. In this case you are better off just doing the task directly.\\n\\n## Examples of When to Use the Todo List\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: I want to add a dark mode toggle to the application settings. Make sure you run the tests and build when you're done!\\nAssistant: I'll help add a dark mode toggle to your application settings. Let me create a todo list to track this implementation.\\n*Creates todo list with the following items:*\\n1. Create dark mode toggle component in Settings page\\n2. Add dark mode state management (context/store)\\n3. Implement CSS-in-JS styles for dark theme\\n4. Update existing components to support theme switching\\n5. Run tests and build process, addressing any failures or errors that occur\\n*Begins working on the first task*\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. Adding dark mode is a multi-step feature requiring UI, state management, and styling changes\\n2. The user explicitly requested tests and build be run afterward\\n3. The assistant inferred that tests and build need to pass by adding \\\"Ensure tests and build succeed\\\" as the final task\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Help me rename the function getCwd to getCurrentWorkingDirectory across my project\\nAssistant: Let me first search through your codebase to find all occurrences of 'getCwd'.\\n*Uses grep or search tools to locate all instances of getCwd in the codebase*\\nAssistant: I've found 15 instances of 'getCwd' across 8 different files. Let me create a todo list to track these changes.\\n*Creates todo list with specific items for each file that needs updating*\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. First, the assistant searched to understand the scope of the task\\n2. Upon finding multiple occurrences across different files, it determined this was a complex task with multiple steps\\n3. The todo list helps ensure every instance is tracked and updated systematically\\n4. This approach prevents missing any occurrences and maintains code consistency\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: I need to implement these features for my e-commerce site: user registration, product catalog, shopping cart, and checkout flow.\\nAssistant: I'll help implement these features. First, let's add all the features to the todo list.\\n*Creates a todo list breaking down each feature into specific tasks based on the project architecture*\\nAssistant: Let's start with user registration. This will involve creating database models, API endpoints, and frontend forms that integrate with your existing authentication system.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. The user provided multiple complex features to implement in a comma separated list\\n2. The todo list helps organize these large features into manageable tasks\\n3. This approach allows for tracking progress across the entire implementation\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Can you help optimize my React application? It's rendering slowly and has performance issues.</user>\\nAssistant: I'll help optimize your React application. First, let me examine your codebase to identify potential performance bottlenecks.\\n*Reviews component structure, render patterns, state management, and data fetching*\\nAssistant: After analyzing your codebase, I've identified several performance issues. Let me create a todo list to track our optimization efforts.\\n*Creates todo list with items like: 1) Implement memoization for expensive calculations in ProductList, 2) Add virtualization for long lists in Dashboard, 3) Optimize image loading in Gallery component, 4) Fix state update loops in ShoppingCart, 5) Review bundle size and implement code splitting*\\nLet's start by implementing memoization for the expensive calculations in your ProductList component.</assistant>\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant used the todo list because:\\n1. First, the assistant examined the codebase to identify specific performance issues\\n2. Based on this analysis, it identified multiple optimization opportunities\\n3. Performance optimization is a non-trivial task requiring multiple steps\\n4. The todo list helps methodically track improvements across different components\\n5. This systematic approach ensures all performance bottlenecks are addressed\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n## Examples of When NOT to Use the Todo List\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: How do I print 'Hello World' in Python?\\nAssistant: In Python, you can print \\\"Hello World\\\" with this simple code:\\n\\npython\\nprint(\\\"Hello World\\\")\\n\\nThis will output the text \\\"Hello World\\\" to the console when executed.</assistant>\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, trivial task that can be completed in one step. There's no need to track multiple tasks or steps for such a straightforward request.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: What does the git status command do?\\nAssistant: The git status command shows the current state of your working directory and staging area. It displays which changes have been staged, which haven't, and which files aren't being tracked by Git. This is a fundamental command for understanding the current state of your repository before making commits.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is an informational request with no actual coding task to complete. The user is simply asking for an explanation, not for the assistant to perform multiple steps or tasks.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Can you add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does?\\nAssistant: Sure, let me add a comment to the calculateTotal function to explain what it does.\\n* Uses the Edit tool to add a comment to the calculateTotal function *\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single, straightforward task confined to one location in the code. Adding a comment doesn't require tracking multiple steps or systematic organization.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n<example>\\nUser: Run npm install for me and tell me what happens.\\nAssistant: I'll run the npm install command for you.\\n\\n*Executes: npm install*\\n\\nThe command completed successfully. Here's the output:\\n[Output of npm install command]\\n\\nAll dependencies have been installed according to your package.json file.\\n\\n<reasoning>\\nThe assistant did not use the todo list because this is a single command execution with immediate results. There are no multiple steps to track or organize, making the todo list unnecessary for this straightforward task.\\n</reasoning>\\n</example>\\n\\n## Task States and Management\\n\\n1. **Task States**: Use these states to track progress:\\n   - pending: Task not yet started\\n   - in_progress: Currently working on (limit to ONE task at a time)\\n   - completed: Task finished successfully\\n   - cancelled: Task no longer needed\\n\\n2. **Task Management**:\\n   - Update task status in real-time as you work\\n   - Mark tasks complete IMMEDIATELY after finishing (don't batch completions)\\n   - Only have ONE task in_progress at any time\\n   - Complete current tasks before starting new ones\\n   - Cancel tasks that become irrelevant\\n\\n3. **Task Breakdown**:\\n   - Create specific, actionable items\\n   - Break complex tasks into smaller, manageable steps\\n   - Use clear, descriptive task names\\n\\nWhen in doubt, use this tool. Being proactive with task management demonstrates attentiveness and ensures you complete all requirements successfully.\\n\\n\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"todos\":{\"type\":\"array\",\"items\":{\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"content\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Brief description of the task\"},\"status\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Current status of the task: pending, in_progress, completed, cancelled\"},\"priority\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Priority level of the task: high, medium, low\"},\"id\":{\"type\":\"string\",\"description\":\"Unique identifier for the todo item\"}},\"required\":[\"content\",\"status\",\"priority\",\"id\"],\"additionalProperties\":false},\"description\":\"The updated todo list\"}},\"required\":[\"todos\"],\"additionalProperties\":false}}},{\"type\":\"function\",\"function\":{\"name\":\"todoread\",\"description\":\"Use this tool to read your todo list\",\"parameters\":{\"$schema\":\"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{},\"additionalProperties\":false}}}],\"tool_choice\":\"auto\",\"stream\":true,\"stream_options\":{\"include_usage\":true}}",
